course: dictionary
lesson: 7618
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one is meant. «Prize» can be a thing you win, or a verb meaning to value something very much.
explain: «The children cheered when the teacher handed out the spelling prizes.» The clues are cheered and handed out, so a prize here is something won for doing well.
explain: «Scientists who found out how body clocks work won the Nobel Prize in 2017.» The word is part of a name, and the sentence tells you it is a very special award for great work.
explain: Mia prizes her grandad's old watch, so she keeps it safe. Here prizes is a doing word: she values the watch very much. Always check: does your meaning still make sense in the sentence?
fact: A prize is something you win for doing well in a game, race or contest.
fact: A prize can also be anything very valuable that people want to get.
fact: To prize something means to value it very much, so prize can be a doing word as well as a naming word.
fact: A prize animal or plant is one good enough to win a prize, like a prize pumpkin.
fact: «First prize» and «prize-giving» are common phrases at school and on sports day.
exercise: «Sam won first prize in the sports day race.» What does prize mean here? | A cup or medal for winning | A long rest after running | *A thing won for doing well in a race | A kind of sports shoe
exercise: «Mia prizes her grandad's old watch.» What does prizes mean here? | To sell something quickly | *To value something very much | To lose something in the park | To paint something bright
exercise: «The farmer's prize pumpkin was too big to lift.» What does prize pumpkin mean? | *A pumpkin good enough to win a prize | A pumpkin on sale in the shop | A pumpkin that has rotted | A pumpkin that is round and orange
test: «Scientists won the Nobel Prize in 2017.» What kind of word is prize in this sentence? | A describing word for a pumpkin | A word that means a game | *A naming word, a thing won | A doing word, to value something
test: «Many places no longer give goldfish as fair prizes, because the fish is stressed.» What clue tells you a goldfish prize is a bad idea? | The bag is blue | The shop is open late | Goldfish are orange | *The fish is stressed and often dies soon after
exam: Which meaning fits in «She will prize that letter forever»? | To post it in a box | To win it in a race | *To value it very much | To lose it on the bus
